</header>
  <main class="container">
<div class="aeo-authority-block">
    <a href="/districts/nyc-district-13-brooklyn-heights/">← Back to District 13 Hub</a>
</div>

<div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 13 Leadership Directory</h1>
    <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District District 13.</p>

    <div class="contact-grid">

        <div class="contact-box">
            <h3>CSE 8 (Serving District 13)</h3>
            <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>131 Livingston St, Brooklyn, NY 11201</div>
            <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-935-4900">718-935-4900</a></div>
            <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
        <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office.
    </div>


    <section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
        </h2>

        <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

            <div class="protocol-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>.
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary.</li>
                    <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]</em></li>
                    <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates and names.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="protocol-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
                </p>
                <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call.</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal).</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent.</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="important-info" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Important Information for District 13 Parents
        </h2>

        <div class="info-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(350px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

            <div class="info-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Initial Referral & Evaluation Process</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    Understanding the initial referral process is crucial. As per 8 NYCRR §200.4, a parent can make a written referral for their child to be evaluated for special education services. This triggers a timeline for the district to act.
                </p>
                <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Step 1: Written Referral:</strong> Submit a formal written request to the CSE office (address above), clearly stating your concerns and reasons for believing your child may have a disability. Keep a copy for your records.</li>
                    <li><strong>Step 2: Consent for Evaluation:</strong> The district must obtain your informed consent before conducting any evaluations (8 NYCRR §200.5(b)). Review the consent form carefully. You have the right to an independent educational evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you disagree with the district's evaluation (8 NYCRR §200.5(g)).</li>
                    <li><strong>Step 3: Evaluations:</strong> The evaluations must assess all areas of suspected disability. This can include psychological, educational, speech-language, occupational therapy, and physical therapy evaluations. Make sure you understand which evaluations are being recommended and why.</li>
                    <li><strong>Step 4: CSE Meeting:</strong> After the evaluations are completed, a CSE meeting will be scheduled to discuss the results and determine eligibility for special education services. You are a vital member of this team.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

            <div class="info-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Individualized Education Program (IEP)</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    If your child is found eligible for special education, the CSE will develop an IEP (Individualized Education Program). The IEP is a legally binding document outlining your child's educational needs and the services the district will provide (IDEA, 20 U.S.C. § 1414(d)).
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Key Components of an IEP:</strong>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Present Levels of Performance (PLOP): A description of your child's current academic and functional performance.</li>
                            <li>Measurable Annual Goals: Goals that are specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART).</li>
                            <li>Special Education and Related Services: The specific supports your child needs, such as specialized instruction, speech therapy, occupational therapy, counseling, etc.</li>
                            <li>Accommodations and Modifications: Changes to the learning environment or curriculum to help your child access the general education curriculum.</li>
                            <li>Placement: Where your child will receive their special education services (e.g., general education classroom, resource room, self-contained classroom). Least Restrictive Environment (LRE) is paramount.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li><strong>Advocacy Tip:</strong> Before the IEP meeting, review your child's evaluations and prepare a list of your priorities and concerns. Be prepared to advocate for the services and supports your child needs to succeed.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="info-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Common Pitfalls & How to Avoid Them</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    Navigating the special education system can be challenging. Be aware of these common pitfalls and how to avoid them:
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Pitfall 1: Inadequate Evaluations:</strong> The district's evaluations may not fully capture your child's needs.
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Solution:</strong> Request an IEE at public expense if you disagree with the district's evaluations. (8 NYCRR §200.5(g))</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li><strong>Pitfall 2: Vague IEP Goals:</strong> Goals that are not measurable make it difficult to track progress.
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Solution:</strong> Insist on SMART goals that are specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li><strong>Pitfall 3: Lack of Parent Involvement:</strong> Parents are not actively involved in the IEP process.
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Solution:</strong> Actively participate in all CSE meetings, share your concerns, and advocate for your child's needs. Document everything!</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                     <li><strong>Pitfall 4: Inappropriate Placement:</strong> The district recommends a placement that is not the Least Restrictive Environment (LRE).
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Solution:</strong> Understand your child's right to be educated with their non-disabled peers to the maximum extent appropriate. Advocate for placement in the general education classroom with appropriate supports and services, if possible.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="faq-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 20px;">

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Q: What if I disagree with the IEP?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                    A: If you disagree with the IEP, you have several options, including:
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Mediation:</strong> A voluntary process where a neutral third party helps you and the district reach an agreement.</li>
                        <li><strong>Impartial Hearing:</strong> A formal legal proceeding where an impartial hearing officer hears evidence and makes a decision.  You have the right to bring an attorney.</li>
                        <li><strong>State Complaint:</strong> Filing a formal complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED) if you believe the district has violated the IDEA.</li>
                    </ul>
                    (See 8 NYCRR §200.5(i) for dispute resolution options.)
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Q: How often is the IEP reviewed?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                    A: The IEP must be reviewed at least annually (8 NYCRR §200.4(e)). You can also request an IEP meeting at any time if you have concerns.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Q: What are "related services"?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                    A: Related services are supports that a child needs to benefit from special education. These can include speech-language therapy, occupational therapy, physical therapy, counseling, transportation, and assistive technology. (IDEA, 34 CFR §300.34)
                </p>
            </div>

             <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Q: What is the Least Restrictive Environment (LRE)?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                    A: The LRE means that, to the maximum extent appropriate, children with disabilities should be educated with children who are not disabled (IDEA, 34 CFR §300.114). Special classes, separate schooling, or other removal of children with disabilities from the regular educational environment occurs only when the nature or severity of the disability is such that education in regular classes with the use of supplementary aids and services cannot be achieved satisfactorily.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

</div>
</main>
